    "https://alfreduniversity.applytojob.com/apply/WHmGpykUnS/HR-Manager-Benefits-Leave-And-Compensation?source=Our Career Page Widget": {
      "title": "HR Manager: Benefits, Leave and Compensation",
      "description": "HR Manager: Benefits, Leave and Compensation - Alfred University - Career Page Skip To Job Description View All Jobs View Our Website Thanks for visiting our Career Page. Please review our open positions and apply to the positions that match your qualifications. To learn more about how Alfred University is Outside of Ordinary, please visit our website. If you need assistance, please contact Tamara Green, by emailing humanresources@alfred.edu or calling 607-871-2965. HR Manager: Benefits, Leave and Compensation Alfred, NY Full Time Human Resources Mid Level Share The HR Manager: Benefits, Leave and Compensation leads the Alfred University’s total rewards programs—including health and welfare, retirement, wellness, and statutory and disability leave programs—and stewards compensation structures through market pricing and equity reviews. The HR Manager: Benefits, Leave and Compensation reports to the Associate Director of HR and supervising one Benefits Specialist. The HR Manager: Benefits, Leave and Compensation this role partners with brokers, carriers, and internal stakeholders to deliver compliant, cost-effective plans, excellent employee service, and data-driven decisions that align with university goals. This is an on-site role. The annual salary range for the HR Manager: Benefits, Leave and Compensation is $70,000 to $75,000, based on qualifications and experience. This is a full-time, exempt position scheduled for 37.5 hours per week. Alfred University offers a comprehensive benefits package for eligible employees, including health insurance, retirement benefits, paid time off, tuition benefits, disability and life insurance, and access to employee wellness and campus resources. Essential Functions Leadership & Supervision Supervises, trains, and directs the work of the HR Specialist (Benefits & Training), providing mentorship, performance feedback, and guidance on complex cases. Participate in the orientation of new HR staff members and cross-training within the HR team to promote consistency and service continuity. Provides quality and timely customer service to employees and supervisors, ensuring professional, courteous, and confidential support. Benefits Administration & Compliance Supervises, trains, and directs the work of the HR Specialist (Benefits & Training), providing mentorship, performance feedback, and guidance on complex cases. Participate in the orientation of new HR staff members and cross-training within the HR team to promote consistency and service continuity. Provides quality and timely customer service to employees and supervisors, ensuring professional, courteous, and confidential support. Ensures timely completion of compliance reporting, including ACA filings, 1095-C forms, and nondiscrimination testing. Leave Management Oversight Serves as the primary administrator for all employee leaves, ensuring communication with the HR Manager for continuity across onboarding and separation processe Works closely with the Associate Director of HR in managing employee leaves, ensuring that all applicable laws and policies are followed and documentation is complete. Oversees administration of FMLA, NY PFL, short-term and long-term disability, workers’ compensation,military, and sabbatical leaves. Coordinates with vendors, payroll, and HRIS to maintain accurate leave tracking and ensure appropriate follow-up with employees and supervisors. Compensation Program Management Conducts market analyses and benchmarking to maintain competitive and equitable compensation structures. Oversees job evaluations, FLSA determinations, and salary range administration in collaboration with the Associate Director of HR. Coordinates the annual salary and merit increase process, providing data analysis and documentation to ensure equity and consistency. Participates in compensation surveys and recommends adjustments based on market and internal data. ​​​​ Data Integrity & Reporting Maintains benefits and compensation data integrity within the HRIS and related systems. Assists with departmental and institutional reporting needs, including benefits utilization, leave trends, and compensation metrics. Partners with HRIS and Payroll to develop dashboards and audit reports that support data-driven decisionmaking. Employee Communication & Training Serves as a subject matter expert for benefits, compensation, and leave programs, providing consultation and training to supervisors and employees. Develop clear, accessible employee communication materials and resources. Assists in the implementation of annual compliance training related to benefits, leaves, and workplace policies. Trains new supervisors on performance documentation related to leaves, pay changes, and position updates. Provides compliance training administration through the Benefits and Training Specialist and coordinates with the HR Operations Manager to ensure training integration into onboarding workflows. Collaboration & University Engagement Collaborates with HR leadership on strategic initiatives related to total rewards and employee well-being. Supports HR-sponsored events and initiatives that foster employee engagement and awareness of University programs and resources. Administer and govern employee benefits programs(health insurance, vision, dental, life/AD&D, disability, EAP, retirement plans, and other employee perks. Administers various leave programs (FMLA, PFL, Short Term Disability, Long Term Disability, ADA interactive process coordination and Workers Compensation claim and return to work policy adherence for all University employees. Qualifications - Education & Experiencem Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Minimum of a Bachelor’s degree is required from an accredited college or university in human resources, business administration, psychology or closely related field. Minimum of (3) three to (5) five years of progressive benefits/compensation experience preferred, including at least (1) one year of supervisory or team lead responsibility. CEBS/CBP preferred, SHRM or PHR certification, preferred Vendor/broker management experience of working in a human resource function with responsibilities that include administration of performance appraisals, workers' compensation, employee leaves and ADA functions. Effective written and oral communication skills. Strong analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Ability to maintain a high level of confidentiality. Proficiency in HRIS and benefits administration systems; experience with PlanSource, NYBEAS, or Banner HR preferred. Institutional Citizenship Demonstrates professionalism, discretion, and respect in all interactions. Supports the University’s commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging through culturally competent and respectful interactions. Contributes to a collaborative culture of mentoring, engagement, and service that supports the success of faculty, staff, and students. Maintains a safe and compliant working environment and participates in required training, including safety protocols. Participates in required and elective professional development, University committees, and initiatives. Maintains a safe working environment within and around their facilities and associated equipment and supplies including, but not limited to, obtaining Safety Data Sheet (SDS) information pertaining to any hazards associated with their work environment. Performs other related duties as assigned to support departmental and institutional goals. About Alfred University Lighting the way for students since 1836.“We've always been a place for makers, especially those who make the future. Our commitment to independent thinking, creativity, academic rigor and community have made us a unique kind of leader since 1836.” Alfred University (AU) was founded on principles of social justice in 1836 by liberal, independent thinkers who placed high value on education for all citizens. The University has retained and built upon the strong values of its founders, developing as an institution of national and international renown that is responsive to the needs of contemporary society while remaining consistent with the spirit of its origins. Alfred University is the second oldest coeducational college in the United States as well as one of the earliest nineteenth century colleges to have enrolled African American and Native American students. It has a long-standing history of educating socially conscious students who make a difference in their professions and their communities. Over the course of the twentieth century, Alfred University evolved into a complex institution offering a full range of programs in the liberal arts and sciences, art and design, engineering, business, education, counseling, and school psychology to its nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students that live, learn, and play on a 232-acre campus located in an idyllic, creative valley in Western New York. To learn more, visit alfred.edu. Alfred University actively subscribes to a policy of equal employment opportunity, and will not discriminate against any employee, student or applicant because of race, age, sex, color, sexual orientation, gender identification or expression, physical or mental disability, religion, ancestry or national origin, marital status, genetic information, military or veteran status, domestic violence victim status, criminal conviction status, political affiliation or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Share Apply Apply for this position Required * First Name * Last Name * Email Address * Phone * Address * Resume * We've received your resume. Click here to update it. Attach resume or Paste resume Attach resume as .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .txt, or .rtf (limit 5MB) or Paste resume Paste your resume here or Attach resume file Cover Letter * References: Please enter names and contact information: * Are you related to anyone currently working at Alfred University? If so, please state who: * Would you require a VISA sponsorship to work for our University? * -- No answer -- Yes No Are you an External or Internal Candidate? * -- No answer -- External Internal The following questions are entirely optional. To comply with government Equal Employment Opportunity and/or Affirmative Action reporting regulations, we are requesting (but NOT requiring) that you enter this personal data. This information will not be used in connection with any employment decisions, and will be used solely as permitted by state and federal law. Your voluntary cooperation would be appreciated. Learn more . Gender Decline to answer Female Male Race/Ethnicity Decline to answer Hispanic or Latino White, not Hispanic or Latino Black or African-American, not Hispanic or Latino Asian, not Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latino American Indian or Alaskan Native, not Hispanic or Latino Two or More Races, not Hispanic or Latino MENA (Middle Eastern and North African) Invitation for Job Applicants to Self-Identify as a U.S. Veteran A “disabled veteran” is one of the following: a veteran of the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service who is entitled to compensation (or who but for the receipt of military retired pay would be entitled to compensation) under laws administered by the Secretary of Veterans Affairs; or a person who was discharged or released from active duty because of a service-connected disability. A “recently separated veteran” means any veteran during the three-year period beginning on the date of such veteran's discharge or release from active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval, or air service. An “active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran” means a veteran who served on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service during a war, or in a campaign or expedition for which a campaign badge has been authorized under the laws administered by the Department of Defense. An “Armed forces service medal veteran” means a veteran who, while serving on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service, participated in a United States military operation for which an Armed Forces service medal was awarded pursuant to Executive Order 12985. Veteran status I IDENTIFY AS ONE OR MORE OF THE CLASSIFICATIONS OF PROTECTED VETERAN LISTED ABOVE I AM NOT A PROTECTED VETERAN I DON’T WISH TO ANSWER Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Form CC-305 OMB Control Number 1250-0005 Expires 07/31/2029 Why are you being asked to complete this form? We are a federal contractor or subcontractor. The law requires us to provide equal employment opportunity to qualified\n                                            people with disabilities. We have a goal of having at least 7% of our workers as people with disabilities. The law says we\n                                            must measure our progress towards this goal. To do this, we must ask applicants and employees if they have a disability\n                                            or have ever had one. People can become disabled, so we need to ask this question at least every five years. Completing this form is voluntary, and we hope that you will choose to do so. Your answer is confidential. No one who\n                                            makes hiring decisions will see it. Your decision to complete the form and your answer will not harm you in any way. If you\n                                            want to learn more about the law or this form, visit the U.S. Department of Labor’s Office of Federal Contract Compliance\n                                            Programs (OFCCP) website at www.dol.gov/ofccp . How do you know if you have a disability? A disability is a condition that substantially limits one or more of your “major life activities.” If you have or have ever had\n                                            such a condition, you are a person with a disability. Disabilities include, but are not limited to: Alcohol or other substance use\n                                                disorder (not currently using\n                                                drugs illegally) Autoimmune disorder, for\n                                                example, lupus, fibromyalgia,\n                                                rheumatoid arthritis, HIV/AIDS Blind or low vision Cancer (past or present) Cardiovascular or heart\n                                                disease Celiac disease Cerebral palsy Deaf or serious difficulty\n                                                hearing Diabetes Disfigurement, for example,\n                                                disfigurement caused by burns,\n                                                wounds, accidents, or congenital\n                                                disorders Epilepsy or other seizure disorder Gastrointestinal disorders, for example,\n                                                Crohn's Disease, irritable bowel\n                                                syndrome Intellectual or developmental disability Mental health conditions, for example,\n                                                depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety\n                                                disorder, schizophrenia, PTSD Missing limbs or partially missing limbs Mobility impairment, benefiting from the\n                                                use of a wheelchair, scooter, walker,\n                                                leg brace(s) and/or other supports Nervous system condition, for example,\n                                                migraine headaches, Parkinson’s\n                                                disease, multiple sclerosis (MS) Neurodivergence, for example,\n                                                attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder\n                                                (ADHD), autism spectrum disorder,\n                                                dyslexia, dyspraxia, other learning\n                                                disabilities Partial or complete paralysis (any\n                                                cause) Pulmonary or respiratory conditions, for\n                                                example, tuberculosis, asthma,\n                                                emphysema Short stature (dwarfism) Traumatic brain injury Please check one of the boxes below: YES, I HAVE A DISABILITY, OR HAVE HAD ONE IN THE PAST NO, I DO NOT HAVE A DISABILITY AND HAVE NOT HAD ONE IN THE PAST I DO NOT WANT TO ANSWER PUBLIC BURDEN STATEMENT: According to the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1995 no persons are required to respond to a collection of information unless such collection displays a valid OMB control number. This survey should take about 5 minutes to complete. You must enter your name and date Name Date Human Check * Submit Application This website uses cookies and other analytics technologies. By selecting \"Allow\", you consent to the recording, use and sharing of your website activity by this website and its service providers. Allow Reject All Powered by"
    },
    "https://alfreduniversity.applytojob.com/apply/0SbTIzeSjy/HR-Specialist-Benefits-Leave-Compliance-Training?source=Our Career Page Widget": {
      "title": "HR Specialist, Benefits, Leave & Compliance Training",
      "description": "HR Specialist, Benefits, Leave & Compliance Training - Alfred University - Career Page Skip To Job Description View All Jobs View Our Website Thanks for visiting our Career Page. Please review our open positions and apply to the positions that match your qualifications. To learn more about how Alfred University is Outside of Ordinary, please visit our website. If you need assistance, please contact Tamara Green, by emailing [email protected] or calling 607-871-2965. HR Specialist, Benefits, Leave & Compliance Training Alfred, NY Full Time Human Resources Mid Level Share The HR Specialist, Beneﬁts, Leave & Compliance Training supports the day-to-day operations of the Human Resources oﬃce by administering employee beneﬁts, leave programs, and required compliance training. Reporting to the Associate Director of Human Resources, this position serves as the primary HR contact for beneﬁt enrollment, beneﬁt-related life events, leave administration, disability coordination, retirement and voluntary beneﬁt processes, and compliance training tracking. This role is responsible for ensuring accurate beneﬁts data, timely employee communication, proper documentation, vendor coordination, beneﬁt invoice reconciliation, and alignment between HR systems, vendor systems, and Payroll. The Specialist partners closely with Payroll, the Manager of HR Operations & Employee Experience, and the HR Specialist, Employee Lifecycle & Experience to support accurate records and a consistent employee experience. This position also participates in new employee orientation by leading the beneﬁts, leave, retirement, voluntary beneﬁts, and compliance training portions of orientation. Rate: $22.25-$23.54, 35 hrs/week Essential Functions: Benefits Administration Administer employee beneﬁt programs, including health, dental, vision, life insurance, disability, retirement, wellness, and voluntary beneﬁt plans. Serve as the primary HR contact for employee beneﬁt questions, plan information, eligibility, enrollment, and beneﬁt-related processes. Process beneﬁt enrollments, changes, terminations, and beneﬁt-related life events in PlanSource, NYBEAS, Banner, and/or other applicable systems. Ensure beneﬁt elections and changes are processed accurately and timely in accordance with plan documents, University procedures, and applicable regulations. Coordinate beneﬁt-related life event documentation, including marriage, birth, adoption, divorce, loss of coverage, and other qualifying events. Support annual open enrollment, beneﬁts renewal activities, employee communications, and vendor coordination under the direction of the Associate Director of Human Resources. Provide employee education regarding beneﬁt options, enrollment deadlines, retirement readiness, wellness resources, and voluntary beneﬁt programs. Maintain accurate beneﬁt records and documentation in accordance with conﬁdentiality expectations and record retention requirements. Leave Administration Administer employee leave programs, including FMLA, New York Paid Family Leave, disability, ADA-related leave coordination, workers’ compensation coordination, and other applicable leave programs. Serve as the primary HR contact for employees and supervisors regarding leave process questions, required documentation, timelines, and return-to-work coordination. Prepare, issue, receive, and track leave-related documentation in accordance with applicable federal, state, and University requirements. Maintain conﬁdential leave records and ensure documentation is complete, accurate, and appropriately retained. Coordinate with Payroll to support proper leave coding, paid time application, disability payments, beneﬁt deductions, and related payroll impacts. Track approved leaves and communicate necessary operational information to supervisors while maintaining employee conﬁdentiality. Partner with and escalate complex leave, disability, ADA-related, return-to-work, or compliance-sensitive matters to the Associate Director of Human Resources for review and direction. Monitor leave timelines and follow up with employees, supervisors, and applicable vendors as needed. Compliance Training Coordinate and track required employee compliance training, including Title IX, harassment prevention, safety, mandated institutional training, and other required programs under support of CHRO. Maintain the University’s training management system and ensure accurate assignment, tracking, reporting, and completion records. Provide support to employees and supervisors regarding training access, completion requirements, deadlines, and reporting. Partner with the HR Specialist, Employee Lifecycle & Experience to ensure required compliance training is incorporated into the new hire onboarding process. Prepare compliance training reports for HR leadership and other University stakeholders as needed. Monitor training completion trends and identify gaps, follow-up needs, or process improvements. Maintain awareness of regulatory or institutional changes that may impact required employee training, beneﬁts, leave programs, or related compliance requirements. Partner with the Associate Director of Human Resources to support beneﬁts, leave, training, and employment compliance reporting, audits, and documentation assigned. Benefits Communication, Orientation, and Employee Education Lead the beneﬁts, leave, retirement, voluntary beneﬁts, and compliance training portions of new employee orientation. Partner with the HR Specialist, Employee Lifecycle & Experience to support a clear, welcoming, and organized new employee orientation experience. Develop and distribute employee communications related to beneﬁts, leave programs, open enrollment, wellness resources, retirement, voluntary beneﬁts, and required training. Maintain accurate and accessible employee resources related to beneﬁts, leaves, retirement, voluntary beneﬁts, and compliance training on HR platforms and the University intranet. Provide timely reminders to employees regarding beneﬁt deadlines, open enrollment, required documentation, leave paperwork, and training completion. Support wellness-related beneﬁt communication and vendor-sponsored wellness initiatives in coordination with HR leadership and beneﬁt vendors. Ensure beneﬁt, leave, and compliance training communications are clear, accurate, employee-centered, and consistent with University procedures. Systems, Data Integrity, Reconciliation, and Vendor Coordination Maintain accurate employee beneﬁts, leave, and training data in Banner, PlanSource, NYBEAS, vendor portals, training systems, and other applicable HR systems. Review employee status changes entered through the employee lifecycle process to determine beneﬁt eligibility impacts. Coordinate with the HR Specialist, Employee Lifecycle & Experience when employment status changes impact beneﬁt eligibility, leave status, payroll deductions, or required training assignments. Reconcile beneﬁt deductions, vendor invoices, and enrollment records to ensure accuracy across HRIS, Payroll, and vendor systems. Process beneﬁt invoices for payment after reconciliation and resolve discrepancies with vendors, Payroll, Finance, or HR leadership as appropriate. Assist with required beneﬁts reporting, including ACA, 1095-C, ERISA-related reporting, retirement plan reporting, and other compliance-related reporting as assigned. Identify and recommend process improvements to strengthen beneﬁts administration, leave tracking, training compliance, reconciliation, and employee communication. Maintain beneﬁts, leave, disability, ADA-related, workers’ compensation, medical, and compliance training records separately from general personnel ﬁles, in accordance with conﬁdentiality requirements and applicable record retention standards. Maintain strict conﬁdentiality of beneﬁts, leave, medical, disability, and employee training records, and ensure information is shared only with individuals who have a legitimate business need to know. Institutional Citizenship Demonstrates professionalism, discretion, and respect in all interactions. Supports the University’s commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging through culturally competent and respectful interactions. Contributes to a collaborative culture of mentoring, engagement, and service that supports the success of faculty, staﬀ, and students. Maintains a safe and compliant working environment and participates in required training, including safety protocols. Participates in required and elective professional development, University committees, and initiatives. Maintains a safe working environment within and around their facilities and associated equipment and supplies including, but not limited to, obtaining Safety Data Sheet (SDS) information pertaining to any hazards associated with their work environment. Performs other related duties as assigned to support departmental and institutional goals. Qualitfications-Education & Experience, Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Minimum of an Associate’s degree required, Bachelor’s degree preferred. At least 2 years of experience in Higher education with an emphasis on beneﬁts administration, compliance training, or HR operations is strongly preferred. Strong understanding of beneﬁts law, regulatory requirements, and leave management. Experience in higher education or public sector HR preferred. Excellent communication and customer service skills with the ability to explain complex information clearly. Proﬁciency in HRIS and beneﬁts administration systems; experience with PlanSource, NYBEAS, or Banner preferred. Strong analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Ability to maintain a high level of conﬁdentiality. Eﬀective written and oral communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively and manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. Physical Demands & Work Environment: The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Personal Protective Equipment must be worn when required.  A respiratory function test and the ability to use respirators may be required.  Stairs and uneven surfaces may be present; artificial lifts, etc., may or may not be available.  The position may require the incumbent to walk outdoors in all types of weather to other buildings or offices located on the campus.  The Campus is in an area that contains numerous hills and slopes and may not always be fully accessible for mobility-impaired individuals. About Alfred University: Lighting the way for students since 1836. “We've always been a place for makers, especially those who make the future. Our commitment to independent thinking, creativity, academic rigor, and community have made us a unique kind of leader since 1836.” Alfred University (AU) was founded on principles of social justice in 1836 by liberal, independent thinkers who placed high value on education for all citizens. The University has retained and built upon the strong values of its founders, developing as an institution of national and international renown that is responsive to the needs of contemporary society while remaining consistent with the spirit of its origins. Alfred University is the second oldest coeducational college in the United States as well as one of the earliest nineteenth-century colleges to have enrolled African American and Native American students. It has a long-standing history of educating socially conscious students who make a difference in their professions and their communities. Over the course of the twentieth century, Alfred University evolved into a complex institution offering a full range of programs in the liberal arts and sciences, art and design, engineering, business, education, counseling, and school psychology to its nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students that live, learn, and play on a 232-acre campus located in an idyllic, creative valley in Western New York. Alfred University actively subscribes to a policy of equal employment opportunity, and will not discriminate against any employee, student or applicant because of race, age, sex, color, sexual orientation, gender identification or expression, physical or mental disability, religion, ancestry or national origin, marital status, genetic information, military or veteran status, domestic violence victim status, criminal conviction status, political affiliation or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Share Apply Apply for this position Required * First Name * Last Name * Email Address * Phone * Address * Resume * We've received your resume. Click here to update it. Attach resume or Paste resume Attach resume as .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .txt, or .rtf (limit 5MB) or Paste resume Paste your resume here or Attach resume file Cover Letter * References: Please enter names and contact information: * Are you related to anyone currently working at Alfred University? If so, please state who: * Would you require a VISA sponsorship to work for our University? * -- No answer -- Yes No Are you an External or Internal Candidate? * -- No answer -- External Internal The following questions are entirely optional. To comply with government Equal Employment Opportunity and/or Affirmative Action reporting regulations, we are requesting (but NOT requiring) that you enter this personal data. This information will not be used in connection with any employment decisions, and will be used solely as permitted by state and federal law. Your voluntary cooperation would be appreciated. Learn more . Gender Decline to answer Female Male Race/Ethnicity Decline to answer Hispanic or Latino White, not Hispanic or Latino Black or African-American, not Hispanic or Latino Asian, not Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latino American Indian or Alaskan Native, not Hispanic or Latino Two or More Races, not Hispanic or Latino MENA (Middle Eastern and North African) Invitation for Job Applicants to Self-Identify as a U.S. Veteran A “disabled veteran” is one of the following: a veteran of the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service who is entitled to compensation (or who but for the receipt of military retired pay would be entitled to compensation) under laws administered by the Secretary of Veterans Affairs; or a person who was discharged or released from active duty because of a service-connected disability. A “recently separated veteran” means any veteran during the three-year period beginning on the date of such veteran's discharge or release from active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval, or air service. An “active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran” means a veteran who served on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service during a war, or in a campaign or expedition for which a campaign badge has been authorized under the laws administered by the Department of Defense. An “Armed forces service medal veteran” means a veteran who, while serving on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service, participated in a United States military operation for which an Armed Forces service medal was awarded pursuant to Executive Order 12985. Veteran status I IDENTIFY AS ONE OR MORE OF THE CLASSIFICATIONS OF PROTECTED VETERAN LISTED ABOVE I AM NOT A PROTECTED VETERAN I DON’T WISH TO ANSWER Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Form CC-305 OMB Control Number 1250-0005 Expires 07/31/2029 Why are you being asked to complete this form? We are a federal contractor or subcontractor. The law requires us to provide equal employment opportunity to qualified\n                                            people with disabilities. We have a goal of having at least 7% of our workers as people with disabilities. The law says we\n                                            must measure our progress towards this goal. To do this, we must ask applicants and employees if they have a disability\n                                            or have ever had one. People can become disabled, so we need to ask this question at least every five years. Completing this form is voluntary, and we hope that you will choose to do so. Your answer is confidential. No one who\n                                            makes hiring decisions will see it. Your decision to complete the form and your answer will not harm you in any way. If you\n                                            want to learn more about the law or this form, visit the U.S. Department of Labor’s Office of Federal Contract Compliance\n                                            Programs (OFCCP) website at www.dol.gov/ofccp . How do you know if you have a disability? A disability is a condition that substantially limits one or more of your “major life activities.” If you have or have ever had\n                                            such a condition, you are a person with a disability. Disabilities include, but are not limited to: Alcohol or other substance use\n                                                disorder (not currently using\n                                                drugs illegally) Autoimmune disorder, for\n                                                example, lupus, fibromyalgia,\n                                                rheumatoid arthritis, HIV/AIDS Blind or low vision Cancer (past or present) Cardiovascular or heart\n                                                disease Celiac disease Cerebral palsy Deaf or serious difficulty\n                                                hearing Diabetes Disfigurement, for example,\n                                                disfigurement caused by burns,\n                                                wounds, accidents, or congenital\n                                                disorders Epilepsy or other seizure disorder Gastrointestinal disorders, for example,\n                                                Crohn's Disease, irritable bowel\n                                                syndrome Intellectual or developmental disability Mental health conditions, for example,\n                                                depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety\n                                                disorder, schizophrenia, PTSD Missing limbs or partially missing limbs Mobility impairment, benefiting from the\n                                                use of a wheelchair, scooter, walker,\n                                                leg brace(s) and/or other supports Nervous system condition, for example,\n                                                migraine headaches, Parkinson’s\n                                                disease, multiple sclerosis (MS) Neurodivergence, for example,\n                                                attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder\n                                                (ADHD), autism spectrum disorder,\n                                                dyslexia, dyspraxia, other learning\n                                                disabilities Partial or complete paralysis (any\n                                                cause) Pulmonary or respiratory conditions, for\n                                                example, tuberculosis, asthma,\n                                                emphysema Short stature (dwarfism) Traumatic brain injury Please check one of the boxes below: YES, I HAVE A DISABILITY, OR HAVE HAD ONE IN THE PAST NO, I DO NOT HAVE A DISABILITY AND HAVE NOT HAD ONE IN THE PAST I DO NOT WANT TO ANSWER PUBLIC BURDEN STATEMENT: According to the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1995 no persons are required to respond to a collection of information unless such collection displays a valid OMB control number. This survey should ta"
